   Ferrari-Souza, Joao Pedro and Aguzzoli, Cristiano S. and Soares,
   Carolina and Abbas, Sarah and Zalzale, Hussein and Povala, Guilherme and
   Lussier, Firoza Z. and Leffa, Douglas T. and Bauer-Negrini, Guilherme
   and Rahmouni, Nesrine and Tissot, Cecile and Therriault, Joseph and
   Servaes, Stijn and Stevenson, Jenna and Benedet, Andrea L. and Ashton,
   Nicholas J. and Karikari, Thomas K. and Tudorascu, Dana L. and
   Zetterberg, Henrik and Blennow, Kaj and Zimmer, Eduardo R. and Souza,
   Diogo and Rosa-Neto, Pedro and Pascoal, Tharick A.},
Title = {Glial reactivity correlates with synaptic dysfunction across aging and
   Alzheimer's disease},
Journal = {NATURE COMMUNICATIONS},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {16},
Number = {1},
Month = {JUL 1},
Abstract = {Previous studies suggest glial and neuronal changes may trigger synaptic
   dysfunction in Alzheimer's disease (AD), but the link between their
   markers and synaptic abnormalities in the living brain remains unclear.
   We investigated the association between glial reactivity and synaptic
   dysfunction biomarkers in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) from 478 individuals
   in cognitively unimpaired (CU) and cognitively impaired (CI)
   individuals. We measured amyloid-beta (A beta), phosphorylated tau
   (pTau181), astrocyte reactivity (GFAP), microglial activation (sTREM2),
   and synaptic markers (GAP43, neurogranin). CSF GFAP levels were
   associated with presynaptic and postsynaptic dysfunction, independent of
   cognitive status or A beta presence. CSF sTREM2 levels were related to
   presynaptic markers in cognitively unimpaired and impaired A beta+
   individuals, and to postsynaptic markers in cognitively impaired A beta+
   individuals. Notably, CSF pTau mediated the relationships between GFAP
   or sTREM2 and synaptic dysfunction. Our findings, validated in two
   independent cohorts (TRIAD and ADNI), reveal a distinct pattern of glial
   contribution to synaptic degeneration.},
